references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   32806 Nov 11 08:32 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov 11 08:06 results -> ../investigation\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:44 revisions\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:44 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:20 source_evidence\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 08:12 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     272 Nov 11 08:12 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent       7 Nov 11 08:06 task -> ../task\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    7460 Nov 11 08:44 validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    6375 Nov 11 08:44 validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   19387 Nov 11 08:44 validation_report.json\n# Paper validation feedback\n\n- generated_at: 2026-11-11T08:44:18.029710+00:00\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t03/claude/20261111_071336/paper`\n- ok: `False`\n\n## Hard errors\n- agentic evidence audit failed: see work/results/t03/claude/20261111_071336/paper/paper_evidence_audit_feedback.json and work/results/t03/claude/20261111_071336/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md\n- figure validation failed: figures[1] figure_type does not match strategy for main_comparison\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `6109`\n- substantive_element_count: `10`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `work/results/t03/claude/20261111_071336/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/t03/claude/20261111_071336/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `60`\n- resolved_references: `24`\n- anchored_references: `36`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `6`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- summary: All numbers and derived quantities match the sealed metrics, derived_metrics.json and the source files, and the qualified outcome is reported correctly. Three wording claims go beyond what the evidence trail supports.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `control-attribution-chiefly-budget` (Results, How much is instance-specific?): The text assigns most of the permutation-retained 0.0216 gain to budget and prior, but no budget-only constant, prior-only variant or per-cell breakdown was evaluated. The paper itself says the control cannot separate budget calibration from dataset or family calibration and that the prior-alone comparison was not run, so \"chiefly\" is an unsupported attribution.\n  - claim: The retained part is knowledge the diagnostic holds about the episode: chiefly the budget, through the prior, and any dataset- or family-level shifts in its simulated values.\n  - evidence: paper.tex Results section on the permuted control, versus paper.tex Discussion (What the control tells us) and the Results sentence that the control does not separate budget from dataset or family calibration; paper_data.json ablation_studies is empty; claims_grounding.json only supports the 0.0216 (61%) / 0.0137 (39%) split via combined_score, skill_vs_always_reliable and control_permuted_score.\n  - requested_fix: Replace \"chiefly the budget, through the prior\" with a hedged statement such as \"episode-level information that may include budget through the prior and dataset- or family-level shifts; the evidence does not apportion it\". Make the later \"consistent with knowing the budget\" sentence match.\n- `held-out-datasets-wording` (Abstract / Introduction / Experimental protocol): One of the three final datasets, German Credit half B (485 rows), is the other hash-parity half of the German Credit data whose half A (515 rows) was a search-plane dataset used to select the configuration. It is disjoint by record hash only, not a separate dataset, and the abstract and introduction do not say so. This could be read as three independent datasets.\n  - claim: On three held-out tabular datasets ... held out from the selection of the diagnostic.\n  - evidence: source_evidence/final_resources_manifest.json (german_B, UCI 144, partition rule hash%2==1) and source_evidence/search_resources_manifest.json (german_A, UCI 144, hash%2==0); paper.tex Supplementary Table tab:data; paper.tex Statistical limits says only \"disjoint ... by record hash\".\n  - requested_fix: In the abstract and introduction, qualify the wording, e.g. \"three datasets disjoint by record hash from the search data, one of which is the complementary half of a search-plane German Credit split\". Note that the German Credit result is not from an independent source dataset.\n- `constants-untuned-abstract` (Abstract / Supplementary Table tab:consts): The abstract's \"hand-set and untuned\" does not mention that the budget-prior constants 0.84 and 0.96 were taken from aggregate label means of earlier search-plane evaluations. The prior is data-derived from evaluator outputs, and the paper's own limitations say search scores informed it. \"Untuned\" overstates how independent the constants are of search-plane label statistics. The supplementary table caption has the same gap.\n  - claim: constants are hand-set and untuned; Constants of the frozen self-diagnostic. None was fitted to instance labels.\n  - evidence: source_evidence/selected_config_NOTES.md (\"Prior values 0.84 / 0.96: aggregate label means by budget seen in earlier evaluation metrics\"); source_evidence/selected_config_program.py lines 35-46 comment; paper.tex Method (provenance paragraph) and Statistical limits; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics mean_label_low_budget and mean_label_high_budget.\n  - requested_fix: Reword the abstract to \"weights and shrinkage constants are hand-set and untuned, and the budget prior was taken from earlier search-plane aggregate label means\". Amend the Table tab:consts caption to say the prior values come from aggregate search-plane label means.\n\n## Soft warnings\n- figures[0].font_sizes.title below minimum: 8.0 < 10.0\n- figures[0].font_sizes.axis below minimum: 8.0 < 9.0\n- figures[1].font_sizes.title below minimum: 8.5 < 10.0\n- figures[1].font_sizes.axis below minimum: 8.5 < 9.0\n- figures[1] PNG width too small: 1674 < 2400\n- figures[2].font_sizes.title below minimum: 8.5 < 10.0\n- figures[2].font_sizes.axis below minimum: 8.5 < 9.0\n- figures[2] PNG width too small: 1674 < 2400\n- figures[2] PNG height too small: 667 < 700\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is well cited on Shapley estimation, value-function semantics, stability measures and tabular data/models. All 26 `\\citep` keys resolve in `references.bib`. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and need no citations. Three narrow gaps justify a targeted retrieval pass.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Run-to-run stability and per-instance uncertainty of sampling-based local explainers (LIME/SHAP).\n- Delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation, and shrinkage/empirical-Bayes estimation.\n- Use of feature attributions in deployed tabular decision settings.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. related-work-instability-comparators (high, comparator) — Related Work\n- **Anchor:** \"Local explanations are known to vary under perturbation and between methods ... Our label isolates a different and narrower source of variation\".\n- **Why:** The paper's core claim is a per-run reproducibility estimate for a stochastic explainer. The related work cites only generic robustness and disagreement papers, plus population-level uncertainty (Williamson2020Efficient). It does not cite work that measures repeated-run instability or per-instance sampling uncertainty of LIME/SHAP-style estimators. These are the closest comparators, and the novelty positioning is thin without them.\n- **Search topics:** stability indices and repeatability of LIME under random sampling; confidence intervals or standard errors for Kernel SHAP and sampled Shapley estimates; reliability of SHAP across repeated runs and budgets.\n\n### 2. method-jackknife-shrinkage-rationale (medium, background) — Method\n- **Anchor:** \"delete-one-group jackknife covariance\"; \"shrink the simulated value toward a budget-specific prior\".\n- **Why:** The diagnostic's two statistical ingredients have no cited source. Both topics were in the original query plan but did not reach the bibliography. One or two foundational references would ground the terminology without changing the story.\n- **Search topics:** delete-a-group jackknife variance estimation; empirical Bayes shrinkage toward a prior.\n\n### 3. intro-deployment-claim (low, positioning) — Introduction\n- **Anchor:** \"Shapley-value attributions are a standard way to justify an individual prediction ... in credit, medical and similar settings\".\n- **Why:** The deployment-practice claim (\"run the explainer once and report what it returns\") is supported only by the original SHAP paper.\n- **Search topics:** explainable ML in deployment, and how practitioners use feature attributions.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Dataset citations (German Credit, Give Me Some Credit, Pima). The datasets are described with sources in the appendix, and extra citations would only add bibliography size.\n- Shapley estimation variants and value-function semantics, which are already well covered.\n- Deep tabular models, which are explicitly out of scope.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0620ed1e-b8e9-439c-b133-0b78f4dcb175","uuid":"1e80416a-24f8-47b3-a9ca-59e6dd55677b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:44:25.043Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 7076\ndrwx------ 9 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:44 .\ndrwx------ 9 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:42 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     751 Nov 11 08:06 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:06 .claude\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 08:42 .concept_figure_complete\nprw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 08:44 .paper_repair_round_