{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018nyR9Cr4QpV847LudtjWo6","type":"tool_result","content":"39:Explanations of tabular predictions are often produced by one run of a stochastic Shapley estimator, and the resulting top features are then reported as the reason for the prediction. We ask when the top-3 features of one budget-limited Kernel SHAP run would be reproduced by independent reruns. We define reproducibility as the mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between the assessed run and eight reruns, and we study coalition budgets of $4d$ and $16d$ for $d$ features. On three held-out tabular datasets and three model families, mean agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$, so single-run reliability depends on the budget. We then test whether the assessed run's own coalition data can predict its reproducibility, using a jackknife-based rerun simulation shrunk toward a budget prior. In the sealed evaluation, which qualified, the predictor lowers squared error against always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines, with positive one-sided bootstrap lower bounds, and beats a pairing-permuted control by 0.0137. The permuted control keeps about 61\\% of the gain over always-reliable, per-instance rank correlation is 0.58, and constants are hand-set and untuned. The results are limited to Kernel SHAP, three datasets and two budgets.\n48:The study has two parts. First, we measure how reproducible single runs are at the two budgets, on three tabular datasets and three model families that were held out from the selection of the diagnostic. Second, we ask whether reproducibility can be \\emph{predicted for a given run from that run alone}. A practitioner holds one run, not eight. A self-diagnostic that reads the run's coalition masks, weights and evaluated coalition values and returns a reproducibility estimate $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ would let the practitioner flag instances whose top-3 set should not be trusted. We evaluate such a diagnostic against three baselines and against a control that keeps all episode-level information but destroys instance-level pairing.\n53:\\item A measurement of budget dependence. Across the held-out datasets and model families, mean top-3 Jaccard agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$ (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n79:Our held-out datasets include an ACS income task from the folktables toolkit \\citep{Ding2021Retiring}. Our model families are logistic regression, random forest and a small MLP implemented in scikit-learn \\citep{Pedregosa2012Scikit}. Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} are not part of this study, and exact tree-specific Shapley algorithms \\citep{Lundberg2018Consistent} are not used. Our framing of reproducibility as a measurable property with explicit scope follows the call for rigorous evaluation of interpretability \\citep{DoshiVelez2017Towards}.\n123:Provenance of the constants matters for interpretation. The weights $0.5/0.2/0.3$, $0.6$, $0.02$, the fold count and the partition count are hand-set and were not tuned on instance labels. The values $0.84$ and $0.96$ are described in the configuration's notes as aggregate label means by budget from earlier search-plane evaluations (\\texttt{selected\\_config\\_NOTES.md}). The shrinkage weight is therefore a fixed constant, not an estimated empirical-Bayes weight; we use the term \\emph{empirical-Bayes-style} only for the structure of shrinking an unreliable per-unit estimate toward a prior derived from other data, as in Stein-type estimators \\citep{Efron1973Stein}.\n129:The evaluation was run once under a sealed protocol, with the selected configuration frozen beforehand. Datasets are the ACS income task (California, 2018, 1-year person records via folktables; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows), Give Me Some Credit (OpenML 46929; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows) and the second half of a hash-based split of Statlog German Credit ($d=20$; 485 rows). Models are scikit-learn logistic regression, a 100-tree random forest and a one-hidden-layer MLP with 64 units, trained on a 70\\% split with train-only median imputation and standardization. For each dataset, family, budget in $\\{4d,16d\\}$ and seed in $\\{9001,9002\\}$ there is one episode of 20 explained test instances, with nine runs per instance (one assessed, eight reruns). That gives 36 episodes and 720 labeled instances. Scores aggregate with equal weight over instances, then seeds within a dataset--family--budget cell, then cells within a dataset, then datasets. The headline quantity is $\\text{score}=1-\\text{MSE}$ of the diagnostic. A \\emph{skill} is the MSE of a comparator minus the MSE of the diagnostic. The evaluator reports one-sided 2.5th-percentile lower bounds from 200 hierarchical bootstrap replicates that resample cells, seeds and instances within each of the three fixed datasets. These datasets were disjoint, by normalized record hash, from the two search-plane datasets (a hash-based split of German Credit and Pima Diabetes) used to choose the configuration (\\texttt{final\\_resources\\_manifest.json}, \\texttt{search\\_resources\\_manifest.json}). The evaluator's pre-declared assessment treats the diagnostic as informative only if the three skill lower bounds are positive and its score exceeds the permuted control by a positive lower bound (\\texttt{task\\_contract.json}, \\texttt{protocol.promotion\\_rule}).\n142:\\caption{Sealed final evaluation. (a) Reduction in mean squared error of the single-run diagnostic relative to each baseline and to the pairing-permuted control, with the evaluator's one-sided 2.5th-percentile bootstrap lower bound (diamonds). (b) Mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between an assessed run and eight reruns at the two budgets, averaged over three held-out datasets, three model families, two seeds and 20 instances per cell.}\n176:In relative terms this is a 45.9\\% reduction in MSE, but in absolute terms it is small, and Figure~\\ref{fig:accounting}b shows why this matters for interpretation. Of the 0.0353 reduction over always-reliable, 0.0216 (61\\%) is retained when the diagnostic's outputs are permuted within episodes, and 0.0137 (39\\%) is lost. The retained part is knowledge the diagnostic holds about the episode: chiefly the budget, through the prior, and any dataset- or family-level shifts in its simulated values. The lost part is the instance-specific information. Both parts are measured, and the lower bound for the instance-specific part is positive, which implies that labels vary across instances in a way that a single run can partly anticipate. The control does not separate budget calibration from dataset- or family-level calibration; the evidence has no breakdown that would allow this.\n190:On the search plane (German Credit half A and Pima Diabetes), the selected configuration scored 0.9854, against 0.9838 on the sealed final data; this search score is a development proxy, was used for selection among three configurations, and is not an independent estimate. The prior constants 0.84 and 0.96 exceed the final label means by 0.0335 at $4d$ and 0.0285 at $16d$ (derived), so the prior was somewhat optimistic on the held-out data, and the diagnostic still qualified. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#budget_prior_minus_final_label]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#best_correct]]\n198:The margin over the permuted control is positive and its lower bound is positive, but 61\\% of the diagnostic's gain over always-reliable survives permutation. Most of the measured benefit is therefore consistent with knowing the budget and similar episode-level facts. The instance-specific contribution is real in the sense of the bootstrap, but small in absolute MSE. A fair comparison would also include a budget-conditional constant, which is the prior alone and was not evaluated; without it, we cannot say how much the simulation adds to the prior.\n207:Only two seeds and three datasets were used. The bootstrap resamples cells, seeds and instances within the fixed datasets, not the datasets themselves, so the lower bounds do not license claims about other datasets. Only one-sided lower bounds were recorded, so we do not report two-sided intervals. The permuted control uses a fixed set of 20 derangements. Search-plane scores informed the choice of the configuration and the prior constants; the final datasets are disjoint from the search datasets by record hash, but they share model families, budgets and evaluation code with the search plane.\n213:For one budgeted Kernel SHAP run on a tabular classifier, reproducibility of the top-3 feature set is conditional on the coalition budget: mean agreement with reruns is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$ across three held-out datasets and three model families. A diagnostic that reads only the assessed run lowers squared error against always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines and against a pairing-permuted control, with positive bootstrap lower bounds, and the sealed evaluation qualified. The conclusion is bounded. The instance-specific gain over the control is 0.0137, most of the gain over the always-reliable baseline survives permutation, rank resolution is moderate (0.58), and neither the contribution of individual components nor results for individual datasets or families were established. The most useful follow-ups follow directly from these gaps: evaluating a budget-only constant and component ablations, reporting per-cell results with two-sided intervals, adding background and retraining variation to the label, and testing a thresholded flagging rule against a stated cost of top-3 errors.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0620ed1e-b8e9-439c-b133-0b78f4dcb175","uuid":"1a114e68-f6d0-45c1-b367-c0272518999c","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:44:30.929Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"39:Explanations of tabular predictions are often produced by one run of a stochastic Shapley estimator, and the resulting top features are then reported as the reason for the prediction. We ask when the top-3 features of one budget-limited Kernel SHAP run would be reproduced by independent reruns. We define reproducibility as the mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between the assessed run and eight reruns, and we study coalition budgets of $4d$ and $16d$ for $d$ features. On three held-out tabular datasets and three model families, mean agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$, so single-run reliability depends on the budget. We then test whether the assessed run's own coalition data can predict its reproducibility, using a jackknife-based rerun simulation shrunk toward a budget prior. In the sealed evaluation, which qualified, the predictor lowers squared error against always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines, with positive one-sided bootstrap lower bounds, and beats a pairing-permuted control by 0.0137. The permuted control keeps about 61\\% of the gain over always-reliable, per-instance rank correlation is 0.58, and constants are hand-set and untuned. The results are limited to Kernel SHAP, three datasets and two budgets.\n48:The study has two parts. First, we measure how reproducible single runs are at the two budgets, on three tabular datasets and three model families that were held out from the selection of the diagnostic. Second, we ask whether reproducibility can be \\emph{predicted for a given run from that run alone}. A practitioner holds one run, not eight. A self-diagnostic that reads the run's coalition masks, weights and evaluated coalition values and returns a reproducibility estimate $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ would let the practitioner flag instances whose top-3 set should not be trusted. We evaluate such a diagnostic against three baselines and against a control that keeps all episode-level information but destroys instance-level pairing.\n53:\\item A measurement of budget dependence. Across the held-out datasets and model families, mean top-3 Jaccard agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$ (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n79:Our held-out datasets include an ACS income task from the folktables toolkit \\citep{Ding2021Retiring}. Our model families are logistic regression, random forest and a small MLP implemented in scikit-learn \\citep{Pedregosa2012Scikit}. Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} are not part of this study, and exact tree-specific Shapley algorithms \\citep{Lundberg2018Consistent} are not used. Our framing of reproducibility as a measurable property with explicit scope follows the call for rigorous evaluation of interpretability \\citep{DoshiVelez2017Towards}.\n123:Provenance of the constants matters for interpretation. The weights $0.5/0.2/0.3$, $0.6$, $0.02$, the fold count and the partition count are hand-set and were not tuned on instance labels. The values $0.84$ and $0.96$ are described in the configuration's notes as aggregate label means by budget from earlier search-plane evaluations (\\texttt{selected\\_config\\_NOTES.md}). The shrinkage weight is therefore a fixed constant, not an estimated empirical-Bayes weight; we use the term \\emph{empirical-Bayes-style} only for the structure of shrinking an unreliable per-unit estimate toward a prior derived from other data, as in Stein-type estimators \\citep{Efron1973Stein}.\n129:The evaluation was run once under a sealed protocol, with the selected configuration frozen beforehand. Datasets are the ACS income task (California, 2018, 1-year person records via folktables; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows), Give Me Some Credit (OpenML 46929; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows) and the second half of a hash-based split of Statlog German Credit ($d=20$; 485 rows). Models are scikit-learn logistic regression, a 100-tree random forest and a one-hidden-layer MLP with 64 units, trained on a 70\\% split with train-only median imputation and standardization. For each dataset, family, budget in $\\{4d,16d\\}$ and seed in $\\{9001,9002\\}$ there is one episode of 20 explained test instances, with nine runs per instance (one assessed, eight reruns). That gives 36 episodes and 720 labeled instances. Scores aggregate with equal weight over instances, then seeds within a dataset--family--budget cell, then cells within a dataset, then datasets. The headline quantity is $\\text{score}=1-\\text{MSE}$ of the diagnostic. A \\emph{skill} is the MSE of a comparator minus the MSE of the diagnostic. The evaluator reports one-sided 2.5th-percentile lower bounds from 200 hierarchical bootstrap replicates that resample cells, seeds and instances within each of the three fixed datasets. These datasets were disjoint, by normalized record hash, from the two search-plane datasets (a hash-based split of German Credit and Pima Diabetes) used to choose the configuration (\\texttt{final\\_resources\\_manifest.json}, \\texttt{search\\_resources\\_manifest.json}). The evaluator's pre-declared assessment treats the diagnostic as informative only if the three skill lower bounds are positive and its score exceeds the permuted control by a positive lower bound (\\texttt{task\\_contract.json}, \\texttt{protocol.promotion\\_rule}).\n142:\\caption{Sealed final evaluation. (a) Reduction in mean squared error of the single-run diagnostic relative to each baseline and to the pairing-permuted control, with the evaluator's one-sided 2.5th-percentile bootstrap lower bound (diamonds). (b) Mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between an assessed run and eight reruns at the two budgets, averaged over three held-out datasets, three model families, two seeds and 20 instances per cell.}\n176:In relative terms this is a 45.9\\% reduction in MSE, but in absolute terms it is small, and Figure~\\ref{fig:accounting}b shows why this matters for interpretation. Of the 0.0353 reduction over always-reliable, 0.0216 (61\\%) is retained when the diagnostic's outputs are permuted within episodes, and 0.0137 (39\\%) is lost. The retained part is knowledge the diagnostic holds about the episode: chiefly the budget, through the prior, and any dataset- or family-level shifts in its simulated values. The lost part is the instance-specific information. Both parts are measured, and the lower bound for the instance-specific part is positive, which implies that labels vary across instances in a way that a single run can partly anticipate. The control does not separate budget calibration from dataset- or family-level calibration; the evidence has no breakdown that would allow this.\n190:On the search plane (German Credit half A and Pima Diabetes), the selected configuration scored 0.9854, against 0.9838 on the sealed final data; this search score is a development proxy, was used for selection among three configurations, and is not an independent estimate. The prior constants 0.84 and 0.96 exceed the final label means by 0.0335 at $4d$ and 0.0285 at $16d$ (derived), so the prior was somewhat optimistic on the held-out data, and the diagnostic still qualified. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#budget_prior_minus_final_label]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#best_correct]]\n198:The margin over the permuted control is positive and its lower bound is positive, but 61\\% of the diagnostic's gain over always-reliable survives permutation. Most of the measured benefit is therefore consistent with knowing the budget and similar episode-level facts. The instance-specific contribution is real in the sense of the bootstrap, but small in absolute MSE. A fair comparison would also include a budget-conditional constant, which is the prior alone and was not evaluated; without it, we cannot say how much the simulation adds to the prior.\n207:Only two seeds and three datasets were used. The bootstrap resamples cells, seeds and instances within the fixed datasets, not the datasets themselves, so the lower bounds do not license claims about other datasets. Only one-sided lower bounds were recorded, so we do not report two-sided intervals. The permuted control uses a fixed set of 20 derangements. Search-plane scores informed the choice of the configuration and the prior constants; the final datasets are disjoint from the search datasets by record hash, but they share model families, budgets and evaluation code with the search plane.\n213:For one budgeted Kernel SHAP run on a tabular classifier, reproducibility of the top-3 feature set is conditional on the coalition budget: mean agreement with reruns is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$ across three held-out datasets and three model families. A diagnostic that reads only the assessed run lowers squared error against always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines and against a pairing-permuted control, with positive bootstrap lower bounds, and the sealed evaluation qualified. The conclusion is bounded. The instance-specific gain over the control is 0.0137, most of the gain over the always-reliable baseline survives permutation, rank resolution is moderate (0.58), and neither the contribution of individual components nor results for individual datasets or families were established. The most useful follow-ups follow directly from these gaps: evaluating a budget-only constant and component ablations, reporting per-cell results with two-sided intervals, adding background and retraining variation to the label, and testing a thresholded flagging rule against a stated cost of top-3 errors.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
