{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RtV2Jm158kwbNm1PeM6GCH","type":"tool_result","content":"39:Explanations of tabular predictions are often produced by one run of a stochastic Shapley estimator, and the resulting top features are then reported as the reason for the prediction. We ask when the top-3 features of one budget-limited Kernel SHAP run would be reproduced by independent reruns. We define reproducibility as the mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between the assessed run and eight reruns, and we study coalition budgets of $4d$ and $16d$ for $d$ features. On three held-out tabular datasets and three model families, mean agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$, so single-run reliability depends on the budget. We then test whether the assessed run's own coalition data can predict its reproducibility, using a jackknife-based rerun simulation shrunk toward a budget prior. In the sealed evaluation, which qualified, the predictor lowers squared error against always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines, with positive one-sided bootstrap lower bounds, and beats a pairing-permuted control by 0.0137. The permuted control keeps about 61\\% of the gain over always-reliable, per-instance rank correlation is 0.58, and constants are hand-set and untuned. The results are limited to Kernel SHAP, three datasets and two budgets.\n48:The study has two parts. First, we measure how reproducible single runs are at the two budgets, on three tabular datasets and three model families that were held out from the selection of the diagnostic. Second, we ask whether reproducibility can be \\emph{predicted for a given run from that run alone}. A practitioner holds one run, not eight. A self-diagnostic that reads the run's coalition masks, weights and evaluated coalition values and returns a reproducibility estimate $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ would let the practitioner flag instances whose top-3 set should not be trusted. We evaluate such a diagnostic against three baselines and against a control that keeps all episode-level information but destroys instance-level pairing.\n226:Table~\\ref{tab:data} lists the datasets, and the remaining protocol constants follow. All values are read from the evaluator source and manifests in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/}.\n230:\\label{tab:data}\n271:\\caption{Constants of the frozen self-diagnostic. None was fitted to instance labels.}\n272:\\label{tab:consts}\n39:Explanations of tabular predictions are often produced by one run of a stochastic Shapley estimator, and the resulting top features are then reported as the reason for the prediction. We ask when the top-3 features of one budget-limited Kernel SHAP run would be reproduced by independent reruns. We define reproducibility as the mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between the assessed run and eight reruns, and we study coalition budgets of $4d$ and $16d$ for $d$ features. On three held-out tabular datasets and three model families, mean agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$, so single-run reliability depends on the budget. We then test whether the assessed run's own coalition data can predict its reproducibility, using a jackknife-based rerun simulation shrunk toward a budget prior. In the sealed evaluation, which qualified, the predictor lowers squared error against always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines, with positive one-sided bootstrap lower bounds, and beats a pairing-permuted control by 0.0137. The permuted control keeps about 61\\% of the gain over always-reliable, per-instance rank correlation is 0.58, and constants are hand-set and untuned. The results are limited to Kernel SHAP, three datasets and two budgets.\n123:Provenance of the constants matters for interpretation. The weights $0.5/0.2/0.3$, $0.6$, $0.02$, the fold count and the partition count are hand-set and were not tuned on instance labels. The values $0.84$ and $0.96$ are described in the configuration's notes as aggregate label means by budget from earlier search-plane evaluations (\\texttt{selected\\_config\\_NOTES.md}). The shrinkage weight is therefore a fixed constant, not an estimated empirical-Bayes weight; we use the term \\emph{empirical-Bayes-style} only for the structure of shrinking an unreliable per-unit estimate toward a prior derived from other data, as in Stein-type estimators \\citep{Efron1973Stein}.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0620ed1e-b8e9-439c-b133-0b78f4dcb175","uuid":"a80696f9-1ca7-45fd-9b44-bc10b98c7ef9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:44:35.779Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"39:Explanations of tabular predictions are often produced by one run of a stochastic Shapley estimator, and the resulting top features are then reported as the reason for the prediction. We ask when the top-3 features of one budget-limited Kernel SHAP run would be reproduced by independent reruns. We define reproducibility as the mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between the assessed run and eight reruns, and we study coalition budgets of $4d$ and $16d$ for $d$ features. On three held-out tabular datasets and three model families, mean agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$, so single-run reliability depends on the budget. We then test whether the assessed run's own coalition data can predict its reproducibility, using a jackknife-based rerun simulation shrunk toward a budget prior. In the sealed evaluation, which qualified, the predictor lowers squared error against always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines, with positive one-sided bootstrap lower bounds, and beats a pairing-permuted control by 0.0137. The permuted control keeps about 61\\% of the gain over always-reliable, per-instance rank correlation is 0.58, and constants are hand-set and untuned. The results are limited to Kernel SHAP, three datasets and two budgets.\n48:The study has two parts. First, we measure how reproducible single runs are at the two budgets, on three tabular datasets